No scores are shown, so I am choosing from what the task needs rather than from results. Placing a brick correctly depends on the landing layer, which is the maximum build height under the brick's footprint. The brick should land where the target has cells across the whole footprint. The footprint-convolution head (r1c2) fits this, so it is probably the strongest base. Three recipe changes on that head are plausible but untested in combination: more PPO updates (r2c1), a long horizon for the end-of-episode bonus (r2c2), and 2 epochs for about 1.9x more fresh designs (r2c3). Small networks run far inside the time limit, so for these networks 2 epochs costs little. This round:
- one candidate combines the two most promising recipe changes;
- one adds a head built on max and min pooling over each footprint after a learned layer, which lets the network work out landing height directly;
- one tests a deeper residual body that sees the whole grid, at about the same compute.
The last two use r2c3's recipe so they can be compared with it.
